#opener("The PiForma was built to be used and carried, with a little care. Its case has already had a long life; the electronics inside would prefer a reasonably quiet one.")
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Handling the computer and display")
#para([#text("Use the carrying handle as described in Chapter 1 and watch the keyboard as the computer comes out of the bag. Avoid impacts, pressure on the display, and pulling on connected cables.")])
#para([#text("The display connections need gentle handling. Do not try to cure a flicker by tapping the case or moving internal parts while powered. Record the symptom and let the builder investigate.")])
]
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Keyboard and mouse care")
#para([#text("Keep crumbs, liquids, and loose items out of the keys. Switch the keyboard off before packing it, and check that Caps Lock is up: a keyboard left on with Caps Lock down keeps sending Shift and runs its battery flat. Pack it with its keys toward the bag wall, thick edge rear, and thin edge front. Turn the mouse off for prolonged storage or transport if desired.")])
#para([#text("The keyboard’s nice!view display shows its battery level, as Chapter 2 explains. Charge it from the USB-C port on its right-hand edge when the level runs low.")])
]
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Charging and storage")
#para([#text("The builder prefers to keep the main battery above roughly 25–50% most of the time rather than repeatedly running it empty. This is a personal usage preference, not a manufacturer storage-charge specification.")])
#para([#text("Use the intended external supply. A full-looking battery may still need the plunger or a brief power connection before startup, as Chapter 1 describes. Bag charging requires the foam alignment and a USB charger on the cord from the bag’s back pocket. The battery display can indicate charging from the wireless receiver, but is hidden while the computer is packed in the bag. Always switch the PiForma off before it goes into the bag; left on, it drains the battery quickly.")])
]
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Stand, bag and transport")
#para([#text("Put the folding stand on a solid flat table, open it securely, and center the PiForma. The stand may permit some side-to-side motion, so keep it away from bumps and do not use it on an unstable surface.")])
#para([#text("Pack in the exact order in Chapter 1. Seat the computer in the bottom foam indents, orient the keyboard correctly, and replace the protective board before the pad, mouse/stand, and pouch. Fasten the clasp as Chapter 1 shows. The bag’s padding helps; it does not justify dropping it.")])
]
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Bose speakers and stands")
#para([#text("The speakers travel separately from the bag. Move the speakers and stands carefully and keep their cables from pulling on the computer. Do not assume the stands can be carried by the speakers or folded while attached.")])
]
#block(breakable:false)[
#heading(level:2,"Cleaning, ventilation and unusual behavior")
#para([#text("Switch off and disconnect charging power before ordinary exterior cleaning. Use a soft dry cloth gently on the case; keep liquids and solvents away from openings and internal parts.")])
#para([#text("To clean the screen, wipe it gently with a washcloth that is only very slightly damp, wrung out until no water drips. No soap is needed.")])
#para([#text("Leave vents clear and keep the PiForma out of direct sun, which will toast it. Watch for unusual heat, smell, swelling, damaged wiring, a flickering screen, or electrical behavior that is out of character. Stop using the machine in that condition. A desktop that still appears is no reason to ignore any of these.")])
#para([#text("The Raspberry Pi sits behind the top left of the screen. To check its temperature, rest a hand on the top left of the case. Warm is normal; if it feels too hot, shut down and switch off. The Pi protects itself by slowing down when it gets very hot, but it doesn’t switch itself off, so it is up to you.")])
]
